gm-method/rendering perf: an instanced-mesh library (@three.ez/instanced-mesh and similar) that exposes per-instance front-to-back SORTING (sortObjects=true + a customSort/radix sort) should have it OFF for OPAQUE instanced geometry. Opaque instances (material with no transparent + no alphaTest -> they write+test depth) get correctness for free from the GPU depth test; sorting only buys a marginal early-Z overdraw reduction, paid for with an O(n) sort over EVERY instance EVERY frame on the rAF (e.g. ~40k grass blades). Turn it OFF for opaque single-geo instanced systems. Keep sort ON only for genuinely TRANSPARENT/alpha-BLENDED instances (back-to-front needed for correct blending) -- and note that on a LOD'd InstancedMesh2 sortObjects=true can additionally DROP the nearest LOD-band instances (a separate known bug), so a LOD'd opaque mesh wants sort OFF doubly. Decide it from SOURCE (read the material's transparent/alphaTest) -- it is a pure-gain CPU win that does not need a live profile to justify when the material is provably opaque. Also: when the managed browser/profiler is unstable in an environment (crashes on heavy page loads), a LIGHT navigation that just fetches the SERVED module text and regexes the invariant still witnesses a client edit, even when a full settled profile is impossible -- and do NOT land unmeasured GPU changes blind; record the profiler-blocked leads as blockedBy:external rather than guessing.